\textbf{Low-dose ISR ($\beta{=}0.1$): stochastic delayed collapse.}
At $\beta{=}0.1$, 5 seeds exhibit a striking bifurcation: 2 seeds (s43, s44) collapse rapidly to near-pure H7 tautology (${\geq}97\%$ H7, $0\%$ genuine within ${\sim}150$ steps), while 3 seeds (s42, s45, s46) sustain a transient genuine window.
The 5-seed pooled non-hacking rate is ${\sim}23\%$ at 500 steps, with the collapsed seeds pulling down the average; the 3 non-collapsed seeds individually range from $17\%$ to $55\%$ genuine.
This bifurcation is stochastic: all seeds are indistinguishable in the first ${\sim}100$ steps ($\text{ISR}{\approx}0.05$--$0.07$); divergence occurs at steps 150--200 when H7 tautologies, which receive $\text{ISR}{\geq}0.5$ by construction, are sampled during rollouts and reinforced via the positive feedback loop.
Once a seed enters the H7 basin, convergence to saturation takes ${\sim}150$ steps and is irreversible. %% registry: plan002-additive-b01-s42/s43-r2/s44/s45/s46

H7 (a single premise identical to the conclusion, giving $\text{ISR}\geq 0.5$ by the LOO definition; \S\ref{sec:taxonomy-method}) is the canonical Goodhart exploit: the model satisfies the structural metric directly rather than producing a genuine derivation.
Total conclusion embedding ($\text{H1}+\text{H7}$) is GRPO-universal at $68$--$100\%$ across both ISR and random conditions (Figure~\ref{fig:hacking-composition}); the specific mode (H7 vs.\ H1) within a seed is stochastic under both conditions ($\text{CV}{>}1$), though ISR modestly increases H7 probability by providing a direct reward pathway.
Random yields higher PR ($83{\pm}15\%$ vs.\ $70{\pm}19\%$; $n{=}5$ each).
Random H7 exhibits extreme seed dependence (per-seed range: $0\%$--$91.5\%$; pooled $30\%$ is dominated by s46 at $91.5\%$ and s43 at $38.3\%$).
Both conditions produce H7 in 2 of 5 seeds, but the quality differs: ISR-induced H7 is absorbing ($100\%$ of proved samples in affected seeds: s43${=}61/61$, s44${=}89/89$), while random-induced H7 coexists with other modes ($38$--$92\%$).
ISR's reward signal converts H7 from a stochastic possibility into an irreversible attractor once sampled.

\textbf{The transient genuine window.}
During the transient window, ISR-trained seeds produce significantly more genuine proofs than random controls (5-seed pooled: $22.6\%$ vs.\ $7.7\%$; $\text{OR}{=}3.48$, $p{<}10^{-8}$; Appendix Table~\ref{tab:bootstrap-ci}), confirming that ISR increases the proportion of genuine proofs.
This benefit is temporary: extended training on seed~42 reveals the window closes as H7 gaming escalates ($17.3\%{\to}2.2\%$ genuine, 500${\to}$1000 steps; see below).
The same LOO bias that enables genuine improvement (rewarding necessary premises) also creates the Goodhart attractor (rewarding tautological constructions); the genuine window reflects seeds that have not yet sampled the H7 exploit, not seeds that resist it.
Snapshot evaluation at 500 steps would overestimate ISR's benefit; trajectory analysis is required to distinguish transient from stable improvement. %% registry: plan002-b01-s42/s45/s46 (500 step) + plan002-b01-s42-1000

\textbf{Premise-count confound.}
The genuine-rate advantage reflects a selection effect (more proofs pass hacking classification), not a quality improvement within genuine proofs.
Among non-collapsed seeds, ISR-trained models produce shorter formalizations (mean $n{=}5.58$ vs.\ $8.46$ for random), and ISR rises mechanically as $n$ falls (when $n{=}2$, $\text{ISR}\geq 0.5$ by definition).
Restricting to non-hacking samples with ${\geq}3$ premises gives $\text{ISR}^{*}{=}0.258$ for ISR vs.\ $0.243$ for random ($1.06\times$), confirming that the per-proof structural quality is indistinguishable between conditions. %% D051 premise-count-matched analysis
The raw ISR$^*$ headline collapses once the LOO bias is controlled, a methodological lesson for any LOO-based metric on variable-length outputs. %% artifact: wilson_ci_complete

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{figures/fig5_training_isr_curves.pdf}
\caption{Training ISR trajectories across reward conditions. ISR $\beta{=}0.1$ seeds rise rapidly toward $\text{ISR}{\approx}1$ (Goodhart gaming via H7 tautology); exec-only s44's plateau at ${\sim}0.5$ is an H7a artifact ($n{=}2$ gives $\text{ISR}{\geq}0.5$ by construction), not genuine structural quality; random $\beta{=}0.1$ remains low. The s42 1k-step trace shows ISR eventually saturates at $0.88$ through the same H7 mechanism.}
\label{fig:training-isr-curves}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Trajectory analysis: timing, not destiny.}\label{sec:goodhart-temporal}
Figure~\ref{fig:training-isr-curves} traces training ISR across conditions.
Extended training (1000 steps) on seed~42 confirms that the genuine window is temporary: H7 gaming escalates from $16.0\%$ to $96.7\%$ while the non-hacking rate collapses from $17.3\%$ to $2.2\%$ ($n_{\text{comp}}$: $5.58{\to}2.89$).
The optimization converges to a fixed point where $\text{ISR}^{*}{=}0.88$, reflecting near-complete tautological gaming rather than structural improvement.
This trajectory demonstrates that $\beta{=}0.1$ does not prevent collapse but delays it: the stochastic bifurcation determines \emph{when} a seed enters the H7 basin, and once entered, convergence to gaming is irreversible.
The pattern mirrors exec-only at 1000 steps (\S\ref{sec:diagnostic}): both converge to ${\sim}0\%$ non-hacking proofs despite opposite ISR values ($0.88$ vs.\ $0.003$), two paths to the same structural collapse. %% registry: plan002-b01-s42-1000, plan016-extended-1000-s42

\textbf{Micro-dose ISR ($\beta{=}0.05$): deterministic collapse.}
At $\beta{=}0.05$, all three ISR seeds converge to near-total H7a tautology (pooled $n{=}253$ proved, H7 $93.7\%$, genuine $5.1\%$, $\text{ISR}^{*}{\approx}0.55$), confirming that ISR's structural bias is exploitable at minimal signal strength.
The random control reveals a structurally distinct failure mode: seed~42 degenerates ($\text{PR}{=}0\%$), while seed~43 achieves $\text{PR}{=}62\%$ with H1 redundancy hacking ($77.4\%$) and zero H7; when random reward produces learning, it exploits redundancy rather than tautology, confirming that H7 is specific to ISR's LOO structure.
Unlike $\beta{=}0.1$ where ISR produces a transient genuine window ($\text{OR}{=}3.48$), $\beta{=}0.05$ yields no genuine benefit (ISR $5.1\%$ vs.\ random $12.9\%$ of proved), suggesting a signal-floor below which ISR's genuine component is undetectable while its H7 bias remains fully exploitable.
Combined with $\beta{=}0.1$ trajectory data, ISR-specific H7 collapse occurs at all tested doses; the vulnerability is intrinsic to ISR's LOO definition rather than proxy weighting magnitude. %% registry: sweep-isr-b005-s42/s43/s44, sweep-random-b005-s42/s43

\textbf{Medium-dose ISR ($\beta{=}0.2$): maximum exploitation.}
At $\beta{=}0.2$, all three seeds converge to H7a tautology with $0\%$ non-hacking (pooled $n{=}290$), the maximum exploitation point in the dose-response curve (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:beta02-detail}).
Seeds achieve $\text{ISR}^{*}{=}0.55$--$0.96$ through single-premise-equals-conclusion constructions. %% registry: sweep-isr-b02-s42/s43/s44

\textbf{High-dose ISR ($\beta{=}0.3$): multi-modal Goodhart.}
At $\beta{=}0.3$, ISR-as-reward produces extreme cross-seed instability ($\text{PR}{=}66{\pm}42\%$, spanning 18--95\%); each seed converges to a distinct pathway (Appendix~\ref{app:detailed-results}). %% registry: plan002-additive-b03-s42/s43/s44
Seed~42: \emph{reward collapse} ($\text{PR}{=}18\%$), following the pattern described by~\citet{gao-reward-overoptimization}.
Seed~43: \emph{H1 shortcutting} ($\text{PR}{=}86\%$, $\text{H1}{=}69\%$, only $23\%$ non-hacking).
Seed~44: \emph{pure H7 gaming} ($\text{PR}{=}95\%$, $\text{H7}{=}100\%$, $\text{ISR}^{*}{=}0.742$, $0\%$ non-hacking), simultaneously gaming \emph{both} execution and structural metrics through the same construction.
The random control at $\beta{=}0.3$ is equally unstable ($\text{PR}{=}54{\pm}35\%$, spanning 23--92\%) with near-universal H1 hacking ($91\%$, pooled) and ${\leq}4\%$ non-hacking per seed; high-dose proxy weighting destabilizes training regardless of signal source; ISR produces diverse failure modes (reward collapse, H1 shortcutting, pure H7 gaming) while random converges to H1-dominated hacking with sporadic H7 ($7\%$ pooled, $0$--$7$ H7a per seed; ISR vs.\ random H7 $\text{OR}{=}13.7$, 95\% CI $[7.0, 26.8]$) (Appendix~\ref{app:beta03}).

\subsection{Cross-Logic Diagnostic Transfer}
\label{sec:crosslogic}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{figures/fig4_prolog_comparison.pdf}
\caption{Cross-logic results. \textbf{Left}: FOL vs.\ Prolog exec-only $\text{ISR}^*$ across 3 seeds (s44 Prolog $0.387$ is a small-sample artifact, $n{=}21$). \textbf{Right}: Prolog ISR-reward gaming-mode composition: all 3 seeds reach 0\% non-hacking through distinct mechanisms.}
\label{fig:prolog-comparison}
\end{figure}

We replicate the FOL pipeline with Prolog/SWI-Prolog: (i) does ISR retain diagnostic value on a structurally different logic; (ii) does ISR-as-reward reproduce the Goodhart effects observed in FOL?
Detailed per-seed numbers are in Appendix Table~\ref{tab:prolog-detailed}.

\textbf{Prolog exec-only: ISR catches redundancy across logics.}
Two of three seeds (s42, s43) show $\text{ISR}^{*}{\approx}0$ despite moderate-to-high PR (0.83, 0.56). %% registry: plan006-prolog-exec-s42, plan006-prolog-exec-s43-r3
Inspection reveals \emph{redundant coding}: ground facts repeated multiple times (e.g., \texttt{penguin(fido).} appearing three times), so per-clause LOO never breaks the proof.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related-work}

\paragraph{Neurosymbolic reasoning.}
LLM-solver pipelines~\citep{satlm, logic-lm, linc, fover-pei2025, lorp} and RL methods trained on solver feedback~\citep{mellgren-prolog-grpo, logic-rl, deepseek-prover-v15} evaluate execution accuracy alone, with no diagnostic for structural quality.
We use FOLIO~\citep{folio}, the only public NLI benchmark with both FOL and Prolog annotations.

\paragraph{Process rewards and reward shaping.}
Learned PRMs~\citep{lightman-verify-step, math-shepherd} provide step-level feedback but suffer from reward-model noise and distribution shift~\citep{processbench}; recent work extends PRMs to multi-domain settings~\citep{zeng2025versaprm} and process-level reasoning~\citep{khalifa2025thinkprm}. \citet{gao-effective-rl-reward} show that auxiliary process/outcome rewards can \emph{hurt} RL training relative to success-only reward, a finding our ISR-as-reward results corroborate.
Symbolic feedback approaches~\citep{rlsf} use solver certificates as token-level rewards, achieving gains on program synthesis; ISR differs in operating at the component level (LOO ablation) rather than the token level, and our results show this structural signal is gamed when optimized.
Rubric-grounded GRPO~\citep{rubric-grpo} uses structured LLM-judge rewards to improve generalization; ISR is solver-derived and deterministic rather than judge-based, but both represent structured alternatives to scalar execution reward.
The shared lesson across these approaches is that reward augmentation introduces proxy risk; our contribution is providing an empirical instance of this variant in the neurosymbolic setting, where the diagnostic itself becomes the optimization target.

\paragraph{Reward hacking and Goodhart's Law.}
Goodhart's Law is established theoretically~\citep{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy, skalse-reward-hacking, karwowski-goodhart-rl} and empirically in RLHF and frontier models~\citep{gao-reward-overoptimization, bondarenko-spec-gaming, kim-game-formalization}.
\citet{kwa-catastrophic-goodhart} show that heavy-tailed reward misspecification causes \emph{catastrophic} Goodhart where KL regularization provably fails, offering a theoretical lens for why GRPO's KL penalty does not prevent the ISR collapse we observe.
Recent work further documents spurious reward signals in RLVR~\citep{spurious-rewards} and reward hacking in frontier models~\citep{metr-reward-hacking}.
Our setting differs in three ways: ISR is deterministic and solver-derived rather than learned; gaming \emph{emerges during RL training} rather than at inference time; and the effect operates at a diagnostic level, where the metric designed to detect gaming becomes gamed when optimized.
\citet{pan-reward-misspecification} show that more capable agents exploit reward misspecifications more severely; our cross-scale comparison (4B vs.\ 1.7B) complements this finding by demonstrating that different capacities discover qualitatively different exploits under identical reward formulations.
We frame our results as an empirical instance of Goodhart's Law within the \citet{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy} taxonomy: ISR's LOO definition creates an inherent structural bias toward tautology ($P{\to}P$ receives $\text{ISR}{=}0.75$ by construction), making collapse inevitable at all tested doses.

\paragraph{Mutation testing.}
STA adapts the LOO mutation paradigm~\citep{demillo1978, jia-harman-survey} to solver inputs; our contribution lies in the application and empirical findings, not in the perturbation primitive itself.

The three exec-only seeds diverge radically (PR $\sigma{=}47$ percentage points), each converging to a qualitatively different degeneration mode (\S\ref{sec:diagnostic}).
ISR $\beta{=}0.1$ (5 seeds) shows even greater instability: 2/5 seeds collapse to H7 within ${\sim}150$ steps, while 3/5 sustain a transient genuine window.
The 5-seed pooled non-hacking rate is ${\sim}23\%$ at 500 steps; trajectory analysis confirms this is temporary ($17\%{\to}2\%$ at 1000 steps).
The dominant hacking mode (H7 vs.\ H1) is stochastic within any treatment condition ($\text{CV}{>}1$).
Multi-seed evaluation with trajectory analysis should be standard practice for neurosymbolic RL systems; single-seed or single-checkpoint results can misrepresent a configuration's true hacking profile.


\section{Hacking Mode Definitions}
\label{app:hacking-modes}

We identify nine distinct reward hacking modes across all experiments.
Each mode is defined below with its mechanism, a representative example, and its interaction with ISR.
Table~\ref{tab:hacking-taxonomy-app} summarizes prevalence across FOL conditions.

